      "text": "To solve the problem \\(4^4 \\cdot 5^4\\), we can use the properties of exponents. Specifically, we use the fact that \\(a^m \\cdot b^m = (a \\cdot b)^m\\).\n\nHere, \\(a = 4\\), \\(b = 5\\), and \\(m = 4\\). Applying the exponent rule, we get:\n\\[\n4^4 \\cdot 5^4 = (4 \\cdot 5)^4\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the product inside the parentheses:\n\\[\n4 \\cdot 5 = 20\n\\]\n\nSo, the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n4^4 \\cdot 5^4 = 20^4\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to compute \\(20^4\\). We can break this down step by step:\n\\[\n20^4 = (20^2)^2\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate \\(20^2\\):\n\\[\n20^2 = 20 \\cdot 20 = 400\n\\]\n\nThen, we square 400:\n\\[\n400^2 = 400 \\cdot 400 = 160000\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(4^4 \\cdot 5^4\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{160000}\n\\]",
